+## Building on a Mac
+
+`scripts/build-games.sh` handles the macOS differences itself, so nothing is manual:
+
+- the `armips` host tool is built with `-std=c++17` (Apple clang defaults to C++98);
+- game preprocessing uses `clang -E -P -x c` (Apple's `cpp` wrapper treats `.mk` files
+  as linker inputs);
+- `sizes.txt` is written portably (the game makefiles use GNU `stat -c`, which macOS
+  lacks, and blank sizes would stop the game loading its data);
+- `scripts/darwin-arm-zstd.sh` checks that `arm-none-eabi-gcc` runs. The darwin-x86_64
+  Arm toolchain links Homebrew's `libzstd`; on a Mac without Homebrew it copies a
+  `libzstd.1.dylib` (for example Anaconda's) into the toolchain folder, rewrites the path
+  to `@loader_path` and keeps backups in `<toolchain>/zstd-fix-backup`.
+
+Each port tree is stamped with its patch and the SDK it was built against. After a toolkit
+update the old tree is moved aside (`build/sm64.old.*`) and objects are rebuilt.
+
+The games use the app/resident shared block (`nanoapps/sdk/hb_shared.h`, ABI v1, from
+olievans123's nano7-untethered): `HB_APP_AUX_BLOCK` holds the game's big heap arena, and
+the hardware-tested resident frees it when the game unloads. The pinned residents already
+implement it, so building a game never changes a resident.
+
+## Fruit Ninja
+
+`fruitninja/` is different: nano7kit's own static recompiler (`tools/recomp.py`, Thumb to
+C), runtime (`rt/`), nano backend (`nano/`) and host harness (`host/`) for the original
+iOS Fruit Ninja 1.0, in the style of olievans123's nano-templerun. `host/fruitninja_rc.py`
+runs it on your own ipa; nothing from the game is stored here. See docs/GAMES.md.
